--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (0/4 key facts kept) ---
I read in the evening paper that the New Year opens gloomily, with more than 2,500,000 men out of work, and the coal and cotton trades in dispute; Cardiff is idle, and London hopes for a temporary arrangement.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (3/3 key facts kept) ---
To-day I spoke with Mr. Walter Thomas about the clerks' room of the Moorgate Fire and Life Assurance Company, and told him that I had been paid my week's wages, £3 10s. I wrote home, telling her of my safety and health, and that I had been paid my wages, and hoped all was well. Mr. Thomas lent me 2s., and I agreed because I have a family, and have been honest and punctual.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Friday, the 2nd of January, 1931 (1/2 key facts kept) ---
1. Set Mr. Hale to balance the quarterly ledger.
